<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oslofjord</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oslofjord is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, stretching about 100 kilometres north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. Its western shore held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> centres of Vestfold, with the ship burials of Oseberg and Gokstad, and its eastern side the district of Vingulmark. At its narrows by Drøbak, Norwegian guns sank the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-cruiser-blucher">German cruiser Blücher</a> in April 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oslofjord.jpg" alt="Oslofjord" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © 2005 by Tomasz Sienicki [tsca, mail: tomasz.sienicki at gmail.com] · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OSLO-NO-04_05_ubt.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Oslofjord begins at the small village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> in Frogn Municipality, runs north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, and then curves east and south until it becomes part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> strait at an imaginary line between the Torbjørnskjær and Færder lighthouses. The Skagerrak connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>, which leads to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. In the geological sense it is not a fjord, but in Norwegian the word covers many kinds of waterway, inlets of this sort among them. The inner and outer fjord are separated by the Drøbak Sound, 17 km long and 1 km wide. The innermost part is Bunnefjorden, and the islands there include Hovedøya, known for monastery ruins, and Gressholmen, known for its rabbits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest settlements around the fjord date from the Stone Age and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Three of the best preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ships, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokstad-ship">Gokstad ship</a>, were unearthed on its eastern and western shores. In historical times the bay was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viken">Viken</a>, the bay, which is the name of the present region. Its Old Norse name was Fold, which gave rise to the counties of Vestfold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostfold">Østfold</a> and the district of Follo. From 1624 until 1925 it was called Christianiafjorden, or Kristianiafjorden, after the capital, whose name was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a>; the Ch became K in 1877.</p><p>The fjord was the scene of a key event in the German invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in April 1940, the Battle of Drøbak Sound. The invasion plan envisaged landing 1,000 troops by ship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>. Colonel Eriksen, commander of the Oscarsborg fortress near Drøbak, which was mainly maintained for historical purposes, sank the German heavy cruiser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> in the narrows. This delayed the rest of the invading force long enough for the royal family, government, parliament and national treasury to leave Oslo, so that Norway never surrendered to the Germans. During the war the Germans had installations at several points on the coast, including one on Hovedøya that held 1,100 soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 1.96 million people live around the fjord including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, most of them on the mainland, although it also holds hundreds of populated islands. The painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-munch">Edvard Munch</a> had a cottage and studio in Åsgårdstrand on its shore, and the fjord appears in several of his paintings, including The Scream and Girls on the Pier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oslofjord” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The word Viking comes from Old Norse, in which a víkingr was a sea raider and víking an expedition overseas; its deeper origin is disputed, perhaps from vík, a bay, or from Viken, the land around the <b>Oslofjord</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Raumarike covered the broad farming lowland of Romerike, north-east of the <b>Oslofjord</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/raumarike">Raumarike</a></li><li><blockquote>Vingulmark covered lands around the northern <b>Oslofjord</b>, roughly modern Østfold and the area around Oslo.</blockquote><a href="/state/vingulmark">Vingulmark</a></li></ul></section>
